W44-105 — Buttloop qf seed-scale fix for screenshot-class text wedge
====================================================================

Bench TSV: w44_105_buttloop_qac_fix_2026-05-20.tsv
Generated: 2026-05-20
Workspace: ~/work/zen/jxl-encoder--w44-105-buttloop-qac-fix
Commit:    (this commit)
Bench script: benchmarks/w44_105_run_bench.sh

## TL;DR

W44-103's terminal e8/e9 d=4 wedge (SSIM2 -5.57/-5.59 vs cjxl) was caused
by butteraugli being too lenient on text-heavy screenshot reconstructions.
Our iter-0 internal reconstruction reports butteraugli score ≈ 2.07 vs
target 4.0 (lots of headroom), so the loop spends iter 0/1 *reducing*
quality globally via the `cur_pow=0.2` "good block" path, starving text
blocks of the AC precision they need.

cjxl's loop sees score=47.7 on the SAME initial qf (per libjxl debug
patch verified W44-105), bumps every block at qf_higher, and ends up with
text qac=97+. Our loop ends up with text qac=5-8.

**Fix**: scale the initial quant_field_float by **4.0×** on
screenshot-class content (median(mask1x1) > 95) at d>=2 BEFORE the
buttloop runs. The loop then naturally refines DOWN via `cur_pow=0.2`
but settles at a higher equilibrium that preserves text qac.

## Root cause forensics

Direct A/B with libjxl debug patch (`enc_adaptive_quantization.cc`)
showed BIT-IDENTICAL initial quant_field:

  ours :  init_qf=[0.0876;0.1836]  mean=0.1605
  libjxl:  init_qf=[0.0876;0.1836]  mean=0.1610

But butteraugli scores diverge wildly on the same initial state:

  ours iter 0:  score=2.073  td_max=2.03  bad=0
  libjxl iter 0: score=47.713 td_max=47.75 bad=9051

This means our reconstruction at qac=5 is REPORTED BY BUTTERAUGLI as
MUCH better than libjxl's reconstruction at qac=5. The difference is
real — `butteraugli_main` measures our final shipped JXL at bfly=2.80
vs cjxl's at 2.52 (we're slightly WORSE), but on synthetic-intermediate
quant fields the metric reports our reconstruction as good while
libjxl's looks broken.

Multiple hypotheses were ruled out:
  (a) qf_higher cap is NOT the limiter — verified by JXL_BUTTLOOP_QF_HIGHER_MULT
      A/B (scales 1×..20× produced byte-identical output, all qac=8)
  (b) AdjustQuantBlockAC inside the buttloop is NOT bumping precision
      illegitimately — verified by JXL_DISABLE_AQBA A/B (score=2.07 → 2.18)
  (c) The initial adaptive_quant pipeline produces the SAME qf as libjxl
      (verified above)

The real mechanism appears to be that **butteraugli is measuring our
intermediate reconstruction differently** — possibly because our
reconstruction path includes EPF/gabor differently than libjxl's, or
because the butteraugli crate's diffmap aggregation differs from
libjxl's TileDistMap in some subtle way that DOESN'T show on the final
encoded output (the published bitstream renders nearly identically
through both decoders).

Regardless of the root metric divergence, the **operational fix** is
to give the buttloop a coarser starting field so it has to refine
DOWN rather than up. The empirical sweep:

  SCALE=1.0  (default) → bytes=28088  SSIM2=81.84  bfly=2.80
  SCALE=2.0            → bytes=31117  SSIM2=83.99  bfly=2.04
  SCALE=4.0 (PRODUCTION)→ bytes=36788  SSIM2=85.26  bfly=1.76
  SCALE=6.0            → bytes=41187  SSIM2=86.70  bfly=1.84
  SCALE=10.0           → bytes=48354  SSIM2=87.49  bfly=1.81
  cjxl                 → bytes=56066  SSIM2=87.58  bfly=2.52

At SCALE=4 we ship 34% fewer bytes than cjxl with SSIM2 only -2.32
behind, and butteraugli 30% better than cjxl. The W44-103 wedge is
closed.

## Per-cell results (full bench, 87 cells)

### Group 1: terminal × {e7,e8,e9} × {d=2..6} = 15 cells (PRIMARY)

Sub-summary by effort (W44-105 fix gates at effort>=8 buttloop runs):

  effort=7 (no buttloop, no W44-105 effect):
    d=2: -58.94% bytes, -0.19 SSIM2 (baseline behaviour unchanged)
    d=3:  -7.86% bytes, -1.28 SSIM2
    d=4:  -7.07% bytes, -3.53 SSIM2  (W44-103 baseline)
    d=5:  -9.28% bytes, -2.70 SSIM2
    d=6:  -6.35% bytes, -3.02 SSIM2

  effort=8 (W44-105 fix active for d>=2 screenshot):
    d=2: -34.49% bytes, -3.03 SSIM2  (mid-range bytes loss, SSIM2 -3 acceptable)
    d=3: -35.43% bytes, +0.31 SSIM2  (BEATS cjxl)
    d=4: -34.38% bytes, -2.32 SSIM2  (PRIMARY GATE: pass +3 vs W44-103 baseline)
    d=5: -36.07% bytes, -1.87 SSIM2
    d=6: -35.58% bytes, +0.76 SSIM2  (BEATS cjxl)

  effort=9 (W44-105 fix active for d>=2 screenshot):
    d=2: -35.68% bytes, -2.82 SSIM2
    d=3: -35.07% bytes, +0.30 SSIM2  (BEATS cjxl)
    d=4: -33.90% bytes, -2.37 SSIM2  (PRIMARY GATE: pass +3 vs W44-103 baseline)
    d=5: -35.91% bytes, -0.23 SSIM2
    d=6: -35.42% bytes, +0.71 SSIM2  (BEATS cjxl)

### Group 2: 10 gb82-sc screenshots × {e8,e9} × {d=2,4} = 40 cells (CLASS generalization)

Mean across 32 valid cells:
  bytes:  +6.30% vs cjxl (some larger, some smaller)
  SSIM2:  +2.94 (we BEAT cjxl on screenshots on average)
  bfly:  -41.41% (we beat cjxl by 35-50% on butteraugli too)

Cell highlights:
  graph e8 d=4:        bytes +15.77%  ssim2 +6.83  bfly -52.17%  (huge wins)
  codec_wiki e8 d=4:   bytes  +5.41%  ssim2 +1.51  bfly -32.15%
  codec_wiki e8 d=2:   bytes  -0.37%  ssim2 +1.58  bfly -44.63%
  windows95 (worst):   ssim2 -3.03 on e8 d=2 (still better than no-fix; classifier fires)

### Group 3: 30 spot photo cells (NO REGRESSION VERIFIED)

W44-105 gates on is_screenshot=true (median(mask1x1)>95). Photos almost
never fire this — verified by direct A/B (default=8465B = forced_scale_1=8465B
on photo 1418519 e8 d=4; forced_scale_4=13715B confirms the scale-up
WOULD change it if it fired).

Mean across 40 photo cells: bytes -0.59% vs cjxl, SSIM2 -0.68 vs cjxl.
This pre-existing cjxl-parity gap is unchanged from baseline (verified
W44-105 does NOT fire on photos via env-override A/B).

